#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151912 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152294 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7640be27-8277-5a9c-bf14-83bcc7a38604" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "58f2bf06-7449-5a66-9662-532ce70a8ff9" :question/math-academy-id "e-21879" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation below, for which the corresponding complementary solution is $y_c(x)=Ax^6+Bx^2,$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants.\n\n$$\nx^2y'' - 7xy' + 12y = -3x^3\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $y_p(x)=x$ or $y_p(x)=x^3$ is a particular solution of the differential equation, and then write the general solution." :question/worked-solution "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y_c(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- $y_p(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For $y_p(x)=x,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}=1, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(1)^{′}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\nx^2(0) - 7x(1) + 12(x) = -3x^3. \\:\\: {\\color{red}\\times}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_p(x)=x$ isn't a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For $y_p(x)=x^3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3})^{′}=3{x}^{2}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(3{x}^{2})^{′}=6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\nx^2(6x) - 7x(3x^2) + 12(x^3) = -3x^3. \\:\\: {\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_p(x)=x^3$ is a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is $y_c(x)=Ax^6+Bx^2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{x}^{6}+B{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151982 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152292 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67ca0bc-c946-5963-a733-2ec9eb6b07ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/id #uuid "bacb284c-88db-5ad5-bd7d-cbe6fbc604df" :question/math-academy-id "e-6789" :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{5x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 10y' + 25y = 0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let $y_{2}=u(x) e^{5x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+5u{e}^{5x}={e}^{5x}({u}^{′}+5u) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={e}^{5x}({u}^{″}+10{u}^{′}+25u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5x}({u}^{″}+10{u}^{′}+25u)-10{e}^{5x}({u}^{′}+5u)+25{e}^{5x}u & =0 \\\\ {e}^{5x}({u}^{″}+10{u}^{′}+25u-10{u}^{′}-50u+25u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{5x}({u}^{″}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{5x}\\neq 0$ we must have\n\n$$\nu'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the equation twice gives\n\n$$\nu(x) = c_1x+c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(x) = ue^{5x}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = e^{5x}\\left(c_1 x + c_2\\right).\n$$\n\nSetting $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = e^{5x}\\cdot x = xe^{5x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W(y_1, y_2) = e^{10x} \\neq 0,$ we know that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the equation is\n\n$$\ny=Ae^{5x}+Bxe^{5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151984 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6302f81d-4834-56a5-b014-0a97c3dccd6f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About General Solutions of Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "bbf72227-f0da-5dab-88cd-bfed2ed773f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-6792" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $y'' - 3y'+ 2y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y_1(x) = e^{x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\nII. $y_2(x) = e^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n\nIII. The general solution of the equation is $y=Ae^{x} + Be^{2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement.\n\n- To determine whether $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y_1$ and its derivatives into the equation. Computing the first and second derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting into the equation, we get\n\n$$\ne^{x}  - 3e^{x} + 2e^{x} = 0. \\color{green}\\quad\\checkmark\n$$\n\nSo $y_1$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of $y_2$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting into the equation, we get\n\n$$\n4e^{2x}  - 3(2e^{2x})  + 2(e^{2x} ) = 0. \\quad\\color{green}\\checkmark\n$$\n\nSo $y_2$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- Both $y_1=e^x$ and $y_2=e^{2x}$ are solutions of the equation. So, the general solution is $y=Ae^{x} + Be^{2x}$ if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent. To check if they are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}{({e}^{2x})}^{′}-{({e}^{x})}^{′}{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian does not equal $0,$ the functions are linearly independent. Therefore, statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct statements are I, II, and III."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151985 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152293 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ef8a6ef-3e77-5e3e-ac75-c3dece0af96e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs With Variable Coefficients"}] :question/id #uuid "f41bad20-5a72-5d6d-82c4-cf36f7ac74cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6794" :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to the equation $x^2 y'' + 3xy' + y = 0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =u(x){y}_{1}(x) \\\\  & =u(x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{u(x)}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y_{2} = \\dfrac{u(x)}{x}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =\\frac{u}{x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}x-u}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & =\\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}x+2u}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(\\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}x+2u}{{x}^{3}})+3x(\\frac{{u}^{′}x-u}{{x}^{2}})+\\frac{u}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}-2{u}^{′}x+2u+3{u}^{′}x-3u+u}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{u}^{″}{x}^{2}+{u}^{′}x}{x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}x+{u}^{′} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $w = u'.$ Then our equation becomes a first-order equation:\n\n$$\nw' x + w = 0\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = \\dfrac{c_1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $w = u',$ we have\n\n$$\nu' = \\dfrac{c_1}{x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = c_1\\ln{x} + c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(x) = \\dfrac{u}{x}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{c_1\\ln{x} + c_2}{x}.\n$$\n\nSetting $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = \\dfrac{\\ln{x}}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W(y_1, y_2) = \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\neq 0,$ we know that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{A}{x} + \\dfrac{B\\ln{x}}{x} = \\dfrac{A + B\\ln{x}}{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6302f81d-4834-56a5-b014-0a97c3dccd6f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About General Solutions of Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0246f7ce-f1b2-5e93-8e63-e7393dc569b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421786} {:answer/id #uuid "70e8c5bf-b371-5452-9286-8ac280de0cca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421797} {:answer/id #uuid "78ece1af-ad7d-5ae8-b110-54b1d28cb817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421798} {:answer/id #uuid "96de1655-9970-5892-87e1-0ac2221ac207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421803} {:answer/id #uuid "fda802d2-36b2-5ed7-834d-f564fbf27833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fda802d2-36b2-5ed7-834d-f564fbf27833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421813} :answer-field/id #uuid "9035e657-b711-5639-a608-83ef4fb511b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fd65812-f6ae-584d-bfe8-d11797aed930" :question/math-academy-id "q-130000" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}-4y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}_{1}(x)=x{e}^{x}$ is a solution to the equation\n\nII. ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation\n\nIII. The general solution of the equation is $y=Ax{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =(1+x){e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =(2+x){e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n(2+x){e}^{x}-4(x{e}^{x})=(2-3x){e}^{x}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is not a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is false.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n4{e}^{2x}-4{e}^{2x}=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- So, ${y}_{1}$ is not a solution while ${y}_{2}$ is a solution of the equation, hence $y=Ax{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}$ is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6302f81d-4834-56a5-b014-0a97c3dccd6f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About General Solutions of Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2573a21a-8e89-50df-979f-28afe2a36547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421792} {:answer/id #uuid "8c15b390-e3ab-578b-9938-8a4a3d2c7655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421801} {:answer/id #uuid "9bc3278d-03ee-5ec6-8887-70c78218b07b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421805} {:answer/id #uuid "9d075126-4b78-5a5b-8783-75c3171a8fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421807} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d3143b-2b4d-55bb-b86b-df87737b53d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c15b390-e3ab-578b-9938-8a4a3d2c7655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421801} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5729148-37a9-5323-892c-ea554a3ec7c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70cf3885-2e2c-5971-b49c-1988c3e0bb2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-130004" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}-2y=0,$ where $x>0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is a solution to the equation\n\nII. ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}$ is a solution to the equation\n\nIII. The general solution of the equation is $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{2}}+B{x}^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}})+2x(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})-2(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(12{x}^{2})+2x(4{x}^{3})-2{x}^{4}=18{x}^{4}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is not a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is false.\n\n- So, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution but ${y}_{2}$ is not a solution of the equation, hence $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{2}}+B{x}^{4}$ is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152293 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ef8a6ef-3e77-5e3e-ac75-c3dece0af96e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs With Variable Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f5c2990-0c8a-584d-bfde-31963f7ff692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=At+Bt\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263135} {:answer/id #uuid "784e1679-3718-5a06-a425-4c1bcafd5944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=At+B{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264044} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9700b6-666a-5f29-a8bb-ff7cd542805c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=At+\\frac{B}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264349} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e28c2f-fe2a-57e6-bebd-5dd0362f05e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=At+\\frac{B}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265126} {:answer/id #uuid "ffcf23b6-c4a2-5b74-ab79-b90a02e66db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=At+B{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d9700b6-666a-5f29-a8bb-ff7cd542805c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=At+\\frac{B}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264349} :answer-field/id #uuid "b667bc8d-3635-50e2-acc5-7b154ab5806c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fa1dae64-83b4-59f1-8ce4-3c9cac8a6ed9" :question/math-academy-id "q-130028" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)=t$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}+2t{y}^{′}-2y=0$ for $t>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t)t.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}t+u \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}t+2{u}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}t+2{u}^{′})+2t({u}^{′}t+u)-2ut & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{3}+2{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut-2ut & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{t}^{3}+4{u}^{′}{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}({u}^{″}t+4{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}t+4{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}t+4w=0.\n$$\n\nThe solution is\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{4}}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3{t}^{3}}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=ut$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3{t}^{3}}+{c}_{2})t.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot t=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=At+\\frac{B}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152294 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7640be27-8277-5a9c-bf14-83bcc7a38604" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cb229fa-6f9c-5a69-b0d6-2b934a5e8256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312323} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ca5e06-743e-56c6-9376-f34c234b3e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313491}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1ca5e06-743e-56c6-9376-f34c234b3e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313491} :answer-field/id #uuid "300f091d-60d2-51e6-9901-b231130a3cf9" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 1759218631413