b/ref 17592186421804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "201d8ac2-9e51-5cf5-98c1-c1ef06a0df77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421790} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f848a61-e820-5e6a-8ef1-bc454ad3d323" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149950} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10f3beec-4202-5dab-a3d5-826a80ea36b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421789} {:answer/id #uuid "243e57c9-66d3-57a3-8eda-0a620df3bf86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421791} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6a2534-2107-5651-a152-891cafe89c13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421799} {:answer/id #uuid "c41c8261-80c9-5a5b-8f42-0d5bf3de5a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "243e57c9-66d3-57a3-8eda-0a620df3bf86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421791} :answer-field/id #uuid "41a0da28-1640-525e-93bc-52f7cb608d46" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "181a7898-7274-5974-9634-f1677406f23f" :question/math-academy-id "q-339880" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}-15y=-7{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-3x},$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{4x}$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{4x}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." :question/worked-solution "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{4x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{4x})^{′}=4{e}^{4x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(4{e}^{4x})^{′}=16{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(16{e}^{4x})-2(4{e}^{4x})-15({e}^{4x})=-7{e}^{4x}.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={e}^{4x}$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{4x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x{e}^{4x})^{′}={e}^{4x}+4x{e}^{4x}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{4x}+4x{e}^{4x})^{′}=8{e}^{4x}+16x{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n(8{e}^{4x}+16x{e}^{4x})-2({e}^{4x}+4x{e}^{4x})-15(x{e}^{4x})=-7{e}^{4x}.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x{e}^{4x}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-3x}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152293 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ef8a6ef-3e77-5e3e-ac75-c3dece0af96e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs With Variable Coefficients"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05fa078e-be12-5c3b-bea0-316dbe5fdadb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{-2}+B{x}^{-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421787} {:answer/id #uuid "07ce657b-e482-5f99-bc7d-7195f7a26f4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{-2}+B{x}^{-2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421788} {:answer/id #uuid "4ebf1486-5cc5-5f08-9b5e-e0e154643134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{-2}+Bx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421795} {:answer/id #uuid "9bfec298-1d17-523e-a8e3-6399a0f68344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{-2}+B{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421806} {:answer/id #uuid "a873b607-3f0b-5c6e-ba8c-a65ec6d7eec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{-2}+Bx\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07ce657b-e482-5f99-bc7d-7195f7a26f4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{x}^{-2}+B{x}^{-2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421788} :answer-field/id #uuid "564f83a4-e64a-5a32-8a58-5a6072793073" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4d0ba009-0a0e-534d-98fc-35f0419611d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-79933" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{-2}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}+4y=0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{-2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{-2}-2u{x}^{-3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{-2}-4{u}^{′}{x}^{-3}+6u{x}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{-2}-4{u}^{′}{x}^{-3}+6u{x}^{-4})+5x({u}^{′}{x}^{-2}-2u{x}^{-3})+4u{x}^{-2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}{x}^{-1}+6u{x}^{-2}+5{u}^{′}{x}^{-1}-10u{x}^{-2}+4u{x}^{-2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}+{u}^{′}{x}^{-1} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x+w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{x}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{x}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{-2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{-2}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{-2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})={x}^{-5}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{x}^{-2}+B{x}^{-2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152292 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67ca0bc-c946-5963-a733-2ec9eb6b07ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3da93a06-f053-5c86-a2c8-81c4330a3717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421793} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffd0753-e57e-5d12-924f-8879651936b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421796} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a79f06-5f3d-5f90-b216-f1e59a6b2bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421809} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6c0735-9902-5936-acbf-abb753290129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421812} {:answer/id #uuid "fdd3af73-a6f5-544b-af96-70b07bb9ad47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3da93a06-f053-5c86-a2c8-81c4330a3717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421793} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c6e7e2e-f610-5676-aec5-d60e36c7c4f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab6bb605-4b84-5240-9d01-8856c6e45ebb" :question/math-academy-id "q-90696" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-3x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-9y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{-3x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-3u{e}^{-3x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-3x}-6{u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+9u{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{″}{e}^{-3x}-6{u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+9u{e}^{-3x}-9u{e}^{-3x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-3x}({u}^{″}-6{u}^{′}+9u-9u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-3x}({u}^{″}-6{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-3x}\\ne 0$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}-6{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}-6w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{e}^{6x}\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{1}{e}^{6x}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{-3x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}(\\frac{1}{6}{c}_{1}{e}^{6x}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=6$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{6x}={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=6\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152292 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67ca0bc-c946-5963-a733-2ec9eb6b07ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "320f4b21-2707-5091-a2f6-3f46b3db3db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355103} {:answer/id #uuid "732019b1-4135-57f2-b0d0-0be5c43e75f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355678} {:answer/id #uuid "7905340a-4ac8-558d-bb3d-307b21001f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355722} {:answer/id #uuid "a886961d-560b-5f03-a7b4-90a1a7508fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356157} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d7e1eb-54ff-5d68-969e-6e99529e477b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Bx\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a886961d-560b-5f03-a7b4-90a1a7508fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356157} :answer-field/id #uuid "296c90ca-5225-52de-bd8b-f92f0038d08f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c4c99c3-e164-55a4-8452-aa0c671a6241" :question/math-academy-id "q-90911" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+9y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=6w\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{sec}^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{sec}^{2}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ becomes \n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=3\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$"}]}
